\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Cross-Judge Replication: Qwen3-32B}
\label{sec:cross_judge}

To test whether the Likert Blindspot generalizes beyond a single judge, we replicate the formality analysis with Qwen3-32B, an open-source model architecturally distinct from GPT-4o.
Qwen3-32B exhibits a 76.4\% pairwise formal preference ($p = 0.0001$), while Likert ATE on the same data is non-significant ($d = 0.011$, $p = 0.908$).
The Blindspot replicates: pairwise comparison detects a clear formality bias that Likert scoring misses entirely.
The higher pairwise win rate (76.4\% vs.\ 63.0\% for GPT-4o) may partly reflect evaluator-producer bias, as Qwen3-32B also serves as the rewriter (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\subsection{Cross-Axis Replication: Verbosity}
\label{sec:cross_axis}

The verbosity axis provides the strongest demonstration of the Blindspot.
Qwen3-32B shows an 87.3\% pairwise preference for verbose content ($p < 10^{-6}$), the largest effect observed in our experiments, while Likert ATE remains non-significant ($d = -0.151$, $p = 0.179$).
Both rewriting directions favor verbose text: the judge prefers the verbose variant whether it is the original or the rewrite, ruling out rewriting-artifact explanations and establishing clean style bias.

\subsection{Negative Control: Register}
\label{sec:negative_control}

Register (academic vs.\ conversational tone) serves as boundary evidence for axis-specific sensitivity.
Qwen3-32B shows a non-significant 59.3\% pairwise preference ($p = 0.220$) and non-significant Likert ATE ($p = 0.505$).
Both measurement methods agree on the absence of register bias, confirming that the Blindspot is axis-specific: pairwise comparison does not hallucinate bias where none exists.

\subsection{Tie Rate as Structural Property}
\label{sec:tie_rate}

Across all four judge--axis configurations, Likert tie rates cluster in the 58--73\% range: GPT-4o formality 62.5\%, Qwen3-32B formality 72.7\%, Qwen3-32B verbosity 58.1\%, Qwen3-32B register 63.8\%.
This consistency across architecturally distinct judges and different style axes supports the interpretation that high tie rates are a structural property of integer Likert scales, not a judge-specific artifact.
The tie rate acts as the first layer of the Blindspot mechanism: before any statistical test is applied, the majority of the evaluation budget is lost to uninformative ties.

