\subsection{Negative Control: Register}
\label{sec:negative_control}

Register (academic vs.\ conversational tone) provides exploratory evidence for a third axis.
The raw pairwise sign test is non-significant (59.3\%, $p = 0.220$) and Likert ATE is also non-significant ($p = 0.505$), but GEE logistic decomposition reveals a statistically significant register effect after controlling for originality ($\text{OR} = 1.91$, 95\% CI $[1.01, 3.58]$, $p = 0.046$; Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
This result should be interpreted cautiously: the confidence interval approaches unity, and the originality confound is the strongest observed ($\text{OR} = 5.72$, $p < 0.0001$), suggesting that register preference may be partly mediated by quality perception differences between academic and conversational text.
Register thus provides a fifth cell of style bias evidence, though as exploratory rather than confirmatory.

